    <string name="onb_title">Before you start</string>
    <string name="onb_intro">Tempo swipes to the next YouTube Short for you, on a timer you choose. To do that it needs Android\'s accessibility service.</string>
    <string name="onb_does_title">What it does</string>
    <string name="onb_does">While a session you started is running, it swipes up in the YouTube app at your interval, and it checks the structure of the screen (not the text) to notice ads, comments and other screens.</string>
    <string name="onb_sees_title">What it can see</string>
    <string name="onb_sees">Only YouTube\'s interface structure: view names, positions and labels used to recognise things like an ad badge. Nothing is stored, logged or sent anywhere.</string>
    <string name="onb_never_title">What it never does</string>
    <string name="onb_never">It never taps ads, never likes, comments or subscribes, never reads titles, comments or usernames, and never hides itself. It only taps YouTube\'s own Skip button if you switch on automatic skipping yourself.</string>
    <string name="onb_control_title">You stay in control</string>
    <string name="onb_control">Sessions have a time limit. A notification with a Stop button is always visible while it runs. You can turn the service off any time in Android settings.</string>
    <string name="onb_consent">I understand and agree</string>
    <string name="onb_step_enable">Turn on the service</string>
    <string name="onb_step_enable_body">Open Android accessibility settings, find Tempo and switch it on.</string>
